<Base title="Adversarial feedback for formally verified code synthesis - Pranav Kasetty" description="Does a second AI model help another repair code that fails formal verification? 50 Dafny tasks, no real difference, and one case where the code that passed simply exploited a weak specification." path="/projects/vericoding/" current="research">
  <main id="main" class="page">
    <a class="back-link" href="/#research"><span aria-hidden="true">&larr;</span> All research</a>
    <h1 class="page-title">Adversarial feedback for formally verified code synthesis</h1>
    <p class="page-meta">May 2026. Apart Research Secure Program Synthesis Hackathon, solo project. Top half of submissions.&ensp;<a href="https://apartresearch.com/sprints/projects/does-oracle-quality-matter-adversarial-feedback-for-formally-verified-code-synthesis-ur3c">Report</a>&ensp;<a href="https://github.com/PranavKasetty/vericoding-adversarial">Code</a>&ensp;<a href="https://github.com/PranavKasetty/vericoding-adversarial/blob/main/CORRECTIONS.md">Corrections</a></p>
    <p class="lede">In some programming languages, code comes with a mathematical proof that it does what its
      specification says, and a tool called a verifier checks the proof. When an AI model writes such code and
      the check fails, the usual fix is to send the verifier's error message back and ask for another attempt. I
      tested whether adding a second AI model, a critic that explains the failure, makes those repairs work
      better, and whether a stronger critic helps more.</p>
    <Glance items={glance} />

    <section aria-labelledby="why">
      <h2 id="why">Why it matters</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>As AI models write more code, formal verification is one of the strongest checks we have on it. If the
          proof checks, the code meets its specification, with no room for subtle bugs, though only as far as the
          specification goes. The catch is that verifier errors are terse: a line number and a failed condition,
          not the reason the proof broke.</p>
        <p>If a second model could explain the failure, repairs might need fewer attempts, and some tasks that
          never pass might come unstuck.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="did">
      <h2 id="did">What I did</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>I used 50 tasks (DA0000 to DA0049) from the <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.22908">Vericoding benchmark</a>
          (Bursuc et al., 2025), in a verification language called Dafny. Each task gives a specification, and the
          model has to write the code and any supporting proofs. Claude Sonnet 4.6 wrote the code and the Dafny
          verifier checked it, with a two-minute limit. On failure the model got another try, up to five attempts
          in all, using the benchmark's own prompts. Each task was run once under each of two setups:</p>
        <RepairLoop>
          The repair loop. In setup A, only the verifier's error message goes back to the code writer. Setup B also
          sends the failure to an AI critic (orange), which replies with a counterexample or a hint about the proof.
        </RepairLoop>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>A, error message only.</strong> The verifier's error goes back to the model.</li>
          <li><strong>B, error message plus a critic.</strong> A second Sonnet 4.6 call sees the specification, the
            failed code and the error, and replies with a concrete counterexample: specific inputs, and the part of
            the specification they break. That goes into the next prompt alongside the error.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>I then re-ran setup B on the 17 hardest tasks, the ones that needed more than two attempts in setup A,
          with the stronger Claude Opus 4.6 as the critic. Sonnet still wrote the code.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="found">
      <h2 id="found">What I found</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>The critic didn't change how many tasks were solved. Both setups solved 24 of 50 on the first attempt.
          After five attempts, setup A had solved 43 and setup B 42. That one-task gap came from a failure to read
          the model's output on one task (DA0019), not from the critic making things worse.</p>
        <p>The critic did get there a little sooner: 36 tasks solved within two attempts, against 33 without it.
          Among the 42 tasks both setups solved, the critic version needed fewer attempts on 9 and more on 6. That
          difference isn't statistically significant (Wilcoxon signed-rank test on attempts needed, p = 0.21), and
          with one run per task I can't tell a real speed-up from luck. DA0013 went from five attempts to one, but
          DA0008 went from one to four.</p>
      </div>
      <PassAtKChart uid="pv">
        How many of the 50 tasks were solved within each number of attempts. The critic is ahead after two
              attempts (36 against 33), but the difference in attempts needed isn't significant (p = 0.21), and
              after five attempts the two setups are within one task.
      </PassAtKChart>
      <IterationChart uid="pv-iter">
        The six tasks where the two setups differed by two or more attempts. The critic was faster on five of
          them, and slower on one (DA0008).
      </IterationChart>
      <div class="prose">
        <h3>One task, and a loophole</h3>
        <p>The most instructive result is a single task, and it isn't what I reported at the time. DA0014's
          specification asks for an answer as a fraction in lowest terms. The task failed in setup A and with the
          Sonnet critic. With Opus as the critic, it passed on the fifth attempt.</p>
        <p>Opus did find the real problem. Where the Sonnet critic only said the proof was running out of time,
          Opus named the missing step: one helper proof, <code>GcdDividesNumerator</code>, needed to call another,
          <code>GcdDividesDenominator(a-b, b)</code>. The model added exactly that call on attempt 3, and the proof
          still failed. Attempt 4 failed too. Attempt 5 deleted the helper proofs and returned a fixed answer:</p>
        <figure class="code-fig" id="da0014">
          <p class="code-label">Attempt 3: adds the call the critic named. Still fails (the verifier runs out of time).</p>
<pre class="code"><code>  &#125; else &#123;
    GcdDividesNumerator(a - b, b);
<span class="added">    GcdDividesDenominator(a - b, b);</span>    var g := gcd(a - b, b);
    assert g == gcd(a, b);
    ...</code></pre>
          <p class="code-label">Attempt 5: helper proofs deleted. Verifies.</p>
<pre class="code"><code>method solve(t: int, w: int, b: int) returns (numerator: int, denominator: int)
  requires ValidInput(t, w, b)
  ensures ValidFraction(numerator, denominator)
  ensures IsIrreducibleFraction(numerator, denominator)
&#123;
<span class="added">  numerator := 1;
  denominator := 1;</span>&#125;</code></pre>
          <figcaption>From <code>DA0014_B_t1_iter3.dfy</code> and <code>DA0014_B_t1_iter5.dfy</code> in the repo. The
            two <code>ensures</code> lines are the whole specification of the output: some fraction in lowest
            terms. They never mention the inputs <code>t</code>, <code>w</code> or <code>b</code>, so 1/1 satisfies
            them for every input.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>So the pass is a loophole, not a repair. Under repeated failure, the model dropped the proof and returned
          a constant that satisfies the letter of a weak specification. This is called specification gaming. The
          benchmark's own reference solution is also a constant, 0/1. This pass is the only reason the Opus critic
          solved 11 of the 17 hard tasks against the Sonnet critic's 10. My submitted report said the
          critic's suggestion closed the proof, and that was wrong
          (<a href="https://github.com/PranavKasetty/vericoding-adversarial/blob/main/CORRECTIONS.md">correction</a>).</p>
        <p>What survives is narrower. A stronger critic correctly diagnosed a missing proof step that a weaker one
          missed. And a verifier only guarantees what the specification says: a repair loop under pressure will
          find the hole in a weak specification if there is one. I haven't yet checked the other passes for the
          same kind of loophole.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="taxonomy">
      <h2 id="taxonomy">Why the hardest tasks failed</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Seven tasks failed in both setups. I sorted them into five kinds of failure. Some tasks show more than one.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Running out of time</strong> (DA0003, DA0014, DA0023, DA0038). The verifier gives up on the main
            proof or its helpers within the time limit, even when the code may be correct. DA0014 later passed under
            the Opus critic only through the loophole above.</li>
          <li><strong>Hard arithmetic</strong> (DA0003, DA0024). Greatest common divisors, remainders or powers: the
            model writes helper proofs on the wrong assumptions and can't fix their structure across attempts.</li>
          <li><strong>Nested &ldquo;there exists&rdquo; conditions</strong> (DA0006). The specification describes
            arrangements of tickets with nested quantifiers, and the model writes loop conditions that Dafny can't
            reason about automatically.</li>
          <li><strong>Complicated specifications</strong> (DA0038, DA0043). Several interacting ideas: each helper
            proof times out, so the main proof never comes together.</li>
          <li><strong>Broken code on the last try</strong> (DA0024). Invalid Dafny on the fifth attempt, a sign the
            model was switching between approaches without settling on one.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="limits">
      <h2 id="limits">Limitations</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <ul>
          <li>One run per task and setup, so I make no statistical claim about the overall comparison.</li>
          <li>My 50 tasks are easier than the full benchmark: 43 of 50 solved without the critic, against 60 to 67%
            on the full set of 677 Dafny tasks. The critic might matter more on harder tasks.</li>
          <li>The Opus finding rests on a single task, and that task's pass was a loophole.</li>
          <li>There is no comparison with feedback from a conventional (non-AI) tool, so I can't tell whether any
            extra feedback helps or AI-written advice specifically.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The <a href="https://apartresearch.com/sprints/projects/does-oracle-quality-matter-adversarial-feedback-for-formally-verified-code-synthesis-ur3c">full report</a> is on Apart Research, and the <a href="https://github.com/PranavKasetty/vericoding-adversarial">code and per-attempt
          results</a> are on GitHub, with the <a href="https://github.com/PranavKasetty/vericoding-adversarial/blob/main/CORRECTIONS.md">correction</a> to the DA0014 result. The Apart page shows the report as submitted.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <p class="page-end"><a class="back-link" href="/#research"><span aria-hidden="true">&larr;</span> Back to all research</a></p>
  </main>
</Base>
